WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.970
- I'm pleased to introduce Merav Shohet

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.050
who is an Assistant
Professor of Anthropology

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.900
at Boston University.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.300
She works at the intersection
of cultural, medical

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.490
and linguistic anthropology,
and has been publishing

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.330
at a rapid clip in all the
top journals in anthropology

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.497
including "American Anthropologist",

00:00:24.497 --> 00:00:26.337
"American Ethnologist",

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:29.530
"The Journal of the Royal
Anthropological Institute"

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.280
and many more prestigious journals.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.100
She's been even writing
about eating disorders

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.870
in North America, although
today it's her work on Vietnam

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.033
which we'll be listening
to in her presentation.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.570
The Vietnam work, which
I'm quite familiar with

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:49.550
is truly pathbreaking.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.597
She's not only interested in care

00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:55.610
and intersubjective
relationships as a topic of study

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.830
but her work is notable for
the care and sensitivity

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.540
she brings into her ethnography.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.820
She engages ethnographically
with Vietnamese concepts

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.510
like sacrifice, hy sinh and
sentiment or love cảm tình

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.150
but in the process, her own work appears

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.310
as a kind of the ethic
laid in careful offering.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:17.870
And I mean that we're careful,

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.830
not only in the sense of being meticulous,

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.950
but as being an act of care itself,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.470
that is her work is full
of care and compassion

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.610
as well as rigorous
linguistic analysis of person

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.930
referencing everyday greetings.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.340
And it's not too often that
you see care and compassion

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.320
in rigorous linguistic
anthropological analysis.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.983
So it's a truly remarkable achievement.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.670
In the subtle but beautifully
rendered stories and episodes

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.480
relayed by Merav's work, we
see how the most mundane,

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.820
everyday acts are imbued with the stuff

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.430
that makes people into people

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.950
and how everyday Vietnamese
engage in the literal work

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.950
of coming into being as
intersubjectively entangled humans,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.584
that beautifully evocative
phrase in Vietnamese,

00:02:07.584 --> 00:02:09.923
(speaks in foreign
language) to make a person.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.240
I'll stop here and please
join me in welcoming Merav

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.720
for today's talk which
will be a discussion

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.280
of her brand new book,
"Silence and Sacrifice:

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.330
Family Stories of Care and
the Limits of Love in Vietnam"

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:26.770
which is just out now

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.700
from the University of California Press.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.083
So please join me in welcoming Merav.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.360
- Gosh, thank you all,

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.620
I think I should let Erik
actually present about my book.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.810
I'm so honored to have you all here

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.640
and to share a bit about my book today.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.573
I'm gonna try to screen share right now.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.010
So I just wanna thank
all of you for being here

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.260
and Erik for inviting me and
giving me this opportunity,

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.763
and Chris, for arranging it.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:05.430
And I'll just launch
right in to talk a bit

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.720
about "Silence and
Sacrifice: Family Stories

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.467
of Care and the Limits
of Love in Vietnam".

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:16.830
And I know that we are all
pretty zoomed out probably,

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.370
it being April and a
year into the pandemic.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.564
So I'll try to keep it not too long

00:03:22.564 --> 00:03:26.683
but do feel free to
interrupt me at any time,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.640
especially in the first part,

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.800
when I'm just talking
about some of the concepts

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.800
of the book, and then I'll
move on to give a few excerpts

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.050
from chapter four of this book.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.730
And the book is really
organized in three parts

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.330
with the introduction setting
up the scene, the concepts,

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.360
and then breaking down
the three basic principles

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.740
that I see as organizing
and structuring life

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.060
and especially kind of life experience

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.590
and ethical subjectivity of
my interlocutors in Vietnam

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.660
and that's sacrifice
asymmetrical reciprocity,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.200
an cam tinh, that we'll be talking about.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.510
And then the second part of
the book emphasizes a lot more

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.380
of the complications on the ground

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.610
especially as people in
Da Nang and Quang Nam

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.810
who had more tenuous
connections to the state

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:30.500
and had kinda ghosts haunting
them at times from the past.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.220
So looking at how these
principles play out in practice

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:35.687
when we study them in Ch2.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:40.090
The research obviously was in Vietnam

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.483
specifically in Da Nang and Quang Nam,

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:47.090
and I conducted the fieldwork a while ago

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.843
in 2002 until 2008.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.160
And then it took me quite a
number of years to process

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.240
all of the data and make sense of it.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.530
So I spent a total of about
18 months in the field

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.513
with 13 consecutive ones in 2007, '08,

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:09.210
and I studied families.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.520
And so I did a lot of household surveys,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.180
kinda looking at family composition

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.300
and routines and that kind of thing,

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.200
and that was about 80 households.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.750
And then among those or including those,

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.513
I studied five focal families,

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:29.760
they we're all extended families,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.760
they were multi-generational,
multi-household,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.390
60% of them lived in
multi-generational units.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.473
Most of them were middle class,

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.360
and this relied on participant
observation on interviews,

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:49.410
on person-centered interviews
except what I came to see it

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:54.160
as because I was looking at
multiple members within a family

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.070
instead of just individuals,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:59.760
I came to see it as a
family-centered ethnography

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.590
which I think enriches the notion

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.690
from psychological
anthropology of person-centered

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:08.350
where you look at people,
not just as tokens of a type

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:13.350
but really as individuals
in their own rights.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.820
I also did a number of recordings,

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:19.070
I went to each family about
every four to six weeks

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:24.070
and in total had 94 hours of recordings,

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.070
which obviously with their permission

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:33.070
and I had thousands of photographs
and notes and all that.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.770
So I'm not sure if I'll ever be able

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:38.180
to transcribe everything
but I did a lot of it

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.460
with the assistance of local assistants

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.323
and then also native speakers
in the U.S. and Canada.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:51.930
And so I did originally mean to study

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:54.800
silence and sacrifice per se,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.040
but really what I was interested in

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.780
is what holds families
together in the face of change.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.820
And that was because in Da Nang

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.770
where the Americans had
landed during the war

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.460
and were situated,

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:11.750
all of the families I
studied had been torn.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:14.450
They had some members who had gone North

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.960
to fight for North Vietnam
and some who had stayed,

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:22.910
some affiliated with the
South, with the Americans,

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.820
some affiliated with Viet Cong,

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.660
some affiliated at times with both.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:32.660
And so I was wondering having
come a good 30 years later,

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:38.650
how is it that families seemed
so able to to eat together

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.060
and celebrate together and walk together

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.713
and enjoy each other's company.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.350
And I found that there
are several key components

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:51.940
that help in this.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.780
One of them is hy sinh or sacrifice

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.350
which I came to think of as
really willingly silently

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.180
and smilingly embracing hardship

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.320
for the benefit of
asymmetrically positioned others.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.150
Vietnam is full of hierarchies,

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:10.683
a lot of it organized
around kinship and cam tinh.

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:15.890
So sacrifice, hy sinh, and
I constantly read about it

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:18.920
and sometimes heard about
it in learning Vietnamese.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.680
And cam tinh is something that
I always heard in daily life

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:23.970
as families talk about it.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.710
And so I really wanted to understand

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:30.470
these very basic concepts,
like what do they mean?

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.250
How do they work?

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.913
How do they help create
the sense of continuity?

00:08:38.264 --> 00:08:42.560
And I came to think of them
as really working together

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:47.430
to also reenforce gender norms

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:52.167
and also age and class
and political divisions.

00:08:54.371 --> 00:08:57.293
And so in thinking about this,

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.760
I was really also interested
in the anthropology of ethics

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:06.390
and morality and how it is
that people enact ethics.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.310
What does it mean to be a virtuous person

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:13.340
in a time of upheaval and
change and rifts and ruptures?

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.810
And I saw the beginning
of the 21st century

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.510
as yet another moment of change

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:23.194
as Vietnam was continuing with
(speaks in foreign language),

00:09:23.194 --> 00:09:26.580
like a market economy where
nationalized forms of care

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.690
were increasingly absent.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:33.390
And I ended up concluding
that despite this ethic

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:37.717
that preaches that people
should be beneficent

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:42.160
to each other, should respect
above and yield below,

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.090
this is what I call
asymmetrical reciprocity,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.980
women especially young and poor ones,

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:50.810
and especially in Da
Nang those who had sided

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:53.640
with the losing side of the American War,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.820
shoulder a disproportionate burden.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.450
And this is in part because
of the market forces

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:02.490
that reinforce the
gender and cultural norms

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.120
that saddle them with the work of cam,

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.963
with putting on the feasts with
navigating the new economy.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:14.107
And so, as I said, a lot of
this has to do with cam tinh

00:10:14.107 --> 00:10:16.820
and hy sinh, sacrifice.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:20.810
And this is really not
gendered in the ways

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:24.010
that maybe we think of in
American or European terms

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:28.200
as sacrifice being the domain
of men to give their lives

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.260
up in war and cam tinh,

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.320
love and care as the domain of women.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.830
Everybody participates
in it, in this logic

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:39.253
but not always in uniformed ways.

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:44.610
And in studying this, I'm
really drawing and expanding

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.250
on certain anthropological concepts,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.930
our approach to sacrifice has
been largely to think of it

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:54.283
in the dramatic terms
as a religious ritual,

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:59.560
as the act of slaughter or substitution

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:03.700
and relatedly as a patriotic act.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:08.040
And both of these are highly
dramatic, a kind of spectacle

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:12.260
with bloodshed and I
think what we have ignored

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:17.260
is really the everyday act
of sacrifice in mundane life.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:19.930
That sometimes I've even gotten push back

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:21.160
when presenting and saying,

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:22.800
well he said this isn't really sacrifice,

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.620
and in fact, in Vietnam
it's very much associated

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:32.610
with patriotic sacrifice
but also with a family

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.660
and with a feminized work of care,

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.390
it's not traditionally
associated with slaughter,

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:40.583
there's other terms for that.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:47.340
And so in looking at the mundane,

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:51.290
I'm looking at sacrifice in
the beginning of the book

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:54.980
as really kinda scaling
these multiple levels

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.530
of both the affiliation with the state,

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:02.530
and in chapter one, I talk
about a man from Quang Nam

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:08.710
who I call Tam, who had
tried to evade conscription

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:14.700
into the French Army in
the 1950s was captured,

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:19.700
was tortured and then in
1954, when Vietnam was split,

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.450
he was released in a prisoner exchange.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:27.450
He went up North and then
spent a good chunk of his life

00:12:28.255 --> 00:12:33.123
away from his natal family, and
then also away from his wife

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:39.280
who he married in the
North, because he went back

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.820
with a call of duty to liberate the South.

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.070
And he talks a lot about sacrifice

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:48.930
and he talks about kind of the
propaganda of Vietnam is one,

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:52.550
nothing is more precious
than liberty and so forth,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:54.920
but what he also talks about is suffering.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:57.230
And one of the things I learned from him

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.610
and from many others is the ways

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.380
that suffering is really
supposed to be silent,

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.500
you don't call attention to yourself

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.820
when taking it on for somebody else.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:12.310
And the ways that men, as well as women

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:15.620
oftentimes attribute sacrifice to women,

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:20.280
especially to mothers, this is
kind of the paragon of virtue

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.070
it is the sacrificing mother
who tends to her children,

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.813
to her husband, to the
nation, to the community.

00:13:30.238 --> 00:13:34.690
And so this kind of
guided my understanding

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:37.330
also of the second principle

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:40.760
which is asymmetrical and reciprocity.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:42.140
And then thirdly, cam tinh

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.770
and the ways that these work together

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:49.380
to organize people's experiences of time

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:53.890
and of their relationships
with the two calendars,

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:56.280
for example, in Vietnam,
with the ancestors,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.043
with each other, with the state.

00:13:59.358 --> 00:14:02.240
And then in chapter three,
I show among other things

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:07.240
how state discourses, and
attempts to legislate equality

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:12.373
are really in effect and
gendering difference.

00:14:13.647 --> 00:14:17.223
And the ways that silence
figures in all of these,

00:14:19.066 --> 00:14:22.770
in naturalizing gender
and other hierarchies.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:25.370
And these are premise of
course, on this presumption

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:29.537
of the unequal, but benevolent
and bi-directional care.

00:14:29.537 --> 00:14:32.860
And so oftentimes when we
think of Vietnam's legacy

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:37.540
as Confucian and as Buddhist
thinking about filial piety

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.540
and some people have written about it

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.680
in terms of a kind of
exploitation from above,

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.750
the power from above demanding respect.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.840
In fact, I think that it's more
useful to think of hierarchy

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.520
as bi-directional in the ideal case.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:55.460
But of course, as I noted earlier

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:58.840
this also doesn't always
play out in practice,

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:03.840
there are those who suffer more
and suffer more in silence.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:07.580
And so then the second half of the book

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:11.440
is traveling some of
these principles of ethics

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.033
and morality of sacrifice
and asymmetrical reciprocity.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:20.780
When I studied them in
practice, looking at families

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:24.280
I think as we need to, if we
want to understand kinship

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:26.770
and relations within a community,

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:30.040
we really need to look
at the multiple people

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.070
with their different and
cross-cutting and dynamic

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:37.780
and really intersectional
perspectives and grievances.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.960
And so I wanna turn now to chapter four

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:44.500
to give a sense of this with
some experts from that chapter.

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:46.230
And just before I do that,

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.410
I wanna introduce three other concepts

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.700
that will help us make sense of these

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:55.700
that I borrow from literary studies.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:59.300
And that really those are the
concepts of foreshadowing,

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:02.240
backshadowing and sideshadowing narratives

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:08.700
that I use in making sense
of people's experiences

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:11.340
and observations and interactions.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.143
So I'm showing you're all
familiar with foreshadowing,

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:20.020
it's where we see past events
as foretelling future ones.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:22.190
So for example, if we
start out something with,

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:25.540
it was a dark and stormy
night, we know that this cliche

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:28.550
is going to lead to
some issues and problems

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:30.380
as is the case in any narrative.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.450
And this is often associated

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:36.380
with narratives of progress and modernity.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:40.420
So in the U.S. the kind of
ideology of manifest destiny

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:43.670
of Americans to move West and conquer

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:47.420
and displace native American peoples,

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:50.010
and in Vietnam too when we think of it

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:53.810
as having the civilization
beginning in the Red River Delta

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:58.760
and moving Southward and
displacing other ethnic minorities

00:16:58.760 --> 00:17:02.500
and forming its own heroic civilization.

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:05.970
And backshadowing uses
the same sort of logic

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:10.600
but in reverse in that we
know already what happened.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:14.060
And then we judge the past
in terms of that knowledge

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.503
of the present, the future.

00:17:16.805 --> 00:17:19.120
And so both the foreshadowing
and backshadowing

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:23.490
tend to have these linear narratives

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.060
with a clear beginning, middle and end

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:32.520
that kind of direct events
with a constant moral stance.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.510
And this is something that
we do all the time in life,

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:38.360
and we oftentimes associate narrative

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:42.000
with these kinds of stories
that come ready-made.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:46.980
But of course, in life
itself when we don't know

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:50.650
what will happen next and
when we often ruminate

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:54.310
on what might have been, what
steps we might have taken

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:56.920
there was also the
practice of sideshadowing.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:01.660
And that is really to
think about contingency

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:08.330
and contradiction, which we
constantly encounter in life

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:12.650
when we feel ambivalence,
when things are not so linear,

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:14.503
when things are not so certain,

00:18:15.666 --> 00:18:19.490
when there is doubt
and incommensurability.

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:23.470
And so I use these
frameworks to make sense

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:28.360
of how people live the
everyday and the ritual lives

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:32.210
and how they make sense of their life.

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:36.520
And in 21st century Da
Nang, when I was there,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.380
two of the things that
really concern people

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:41.150
were of course, health and wealth.

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:45.040
And those are things that
are sometimes precarious

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:50.200
to have especially in
a marketizing economy

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:52.980
although really in any economy.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:56.170
And which I saw as that even though people

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:58.070
were not exactly eager

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.840
to talk about the past most of the time,

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:05.370
there was really a kind of
ideology of let's move beyond

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:08.080
and let's not think
about the painful past.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:13.080
At the same time, there was
a kind of long shadow cast

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:18.320
by war when people's
loyalties had been split

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:23.320
and also concurrently the
uncertainty about the future

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:28.191
during that present transition
to market socialism,

00:19:28.191 --> 00:19:31.460
(speaks in foreign language)
and so one of the things

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:34.440
I wanna argue is that there
was really no such thing

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:36.930
as privatization which
(speaks in foreign language)

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:41.930
is oftentimes in neoliberalism
is associated with,

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:49.030
by looking and using the tools
of linguistic anthropology

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:53.643
to highlight the micro instances of care.

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:57.340
And here I focus on the moral

00:19:57.340 --> 00:19:59.860
rather than the political ecology

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:01.940
of market socialist Vietnam

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:04.390
and illustrate some of the ambivalences

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:07.090
and complexities of care.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.740
And here I begin with two premises.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:14.020
One is that in focusing
on individuals who care,

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.840
I assume not the rightfully maligned

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:22.220
autonomous bounded self-interested persons

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:24.550
that the U.S. usually attribute to Western

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:26.710
or even neoliberal subject

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:31.050
but instead I'm thinking
of persons as relational,

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:36.050
as evaluative subjects, whose
ethics, they come into relief

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.230
when we attend to the living narratives

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:40.270
of everyday intervention.

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.610
And here again, what I mean by narrative

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:44.490
is not a contained text

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:47.840
but rather how people talk with another

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:52.800
and interact one another to make sense

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.580
and to lay moral claims
against one another

00:20:56.450 --> 00:21:00.530
as these unfold in time
and in interactions.

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:05.240
And what else too was that
peoples and family caregivers

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:09.950
attempts to provide care is
really fraught with moral peril

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.860
and filled with ambivalent silences

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:15.240
that threaten to unravel the relations

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:16.910
in late reform Vietnam.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.600
And so any harmony or continuity

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:22.730
or kind of sense of the ordinary

00:21:22.730 --> 00:21:27.210
that we might think of,
these are not ready-made,

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.630
these are effortfully achieved

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:32.270
and they're precariously sustained.

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.330
And my second assumption is that

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:38.533
in cases of end of life
care, we need competence.

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:43.900
Caregivers, they're constantly striving

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.540
in an effort to keep the
dying alive and comfortable.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:50.270
And yet this competence or incompetence

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:52.680
is at once imminent, it's impending

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:57.460
since we can become incompetent
or competent at any time,

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:58.530
but it's also imminent.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:00.580
It's inherent in any social interaction,

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:02.443
and that's why it's also fluid.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:08.750
And throughout fieldwork,
what I sought to understand

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:10.217
is what it means

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:21.150
to be a competent ethical
person in Da Nang.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:23.215
And I've already published on this,

00:22:23.215 --> 00:22:24.570
and it also figures in chapter two,

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:27.140
the ways that children
learn politeness routines

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:29.560
to acknowledge their elders,

00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:31.970
and this is actually an incipient form

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:34.590
of subjectivation into the morality,

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:36.910
into the disposition of sacrifice

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:41.890
that has a lot to do with
ancestral worship later in life

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:45.560
and ways of taking on
suffering in silence,

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:50.560
disciplining the body
withholding from yourself.

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.830
And these acts are never
private, they're never unitary.

00:22:53.830 --> 00:22:58.170
They involve a range of
asymmetrical forms of reciprocity

00:22:58.170 --> 00:23:00.650
and they reinforce hierarchies.

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:04.600
And so here I focus on one such instance

00:23:06.505 --> 00:23:08.105
and the stories that surrounded,

00:23:11.224 --> 00:23:13.120
not in a case of childcare,

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.563
but really in a case of older care,

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:18.690
where unlike in America where oftentimes

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:21.707
we think of the end of life
as highly biomedicalized

00:23:21.707 --> 00:23:24.370
and this of course exists,
people do go to the hospital,

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:27.130
the person I talk about
did go to the hospital

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:32.130
but ultimately a lot of the
care is done at home by kin

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:37.530
who have to also continue
their daily life,

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:39.480
their worship activities,

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:42.930
their income earning
activities and all that.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:46.760
And so I focus here on a 75-year old woman

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:51.760
who I call Ba Bay, and she
started complaining to me quietly

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:55.280
back in April of headaches.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:59.330
And then she suffered minor stroke in July

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.180
and then spent weeks in the hospital

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:05.050
and finally returned
home and went right back

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:08.950
to the squatting of food
preparation and cleaning

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:11.890
and taking care of her granddaughters,

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:14.430
and she suffered another stroke.

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:18.940
And this time she went into
a permanent vegetative state

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:22.983
and the family was essentially
waiting for her to die.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.650
But they didn't know when she might die,

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:32.560
and they shrouded this
calamity in silence.

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:36.900
So Ba's husband, Ong, he
coped by sleeping a lot

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:41.470
and saying very little,
and the families women

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:45.140
were the ones who really
stepped in to fill the vacuum

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:49.890
and manage the loss with
material acts of caregiving.

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:54.830
And so again, she spent
several months in the hospital

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:57.940
hooked up to machines and then in the end,

00:24:57.940 --> 00:24:59.410
in the face of mounting costs,

00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:02.110
she returned home in December.

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:04.570
And so she lay in the bed,
placed in the living room

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:07.573
tended by her daughters
and daughters-in-law,

00:25:08.963 --> 00:25:11.070
and what I wanna focus here right now

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:13.700
is on just a minute of interaction,

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:16.173
and I'll just show maybe
half a minute of it,

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:20.750
of footage that I filmed in January,

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:23.690
about a month after
she had come back home.

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:27.900
And one of my methodologies
was filming regularly

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:30.920
in the family homes and
I admit I was reluctant

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:36.890
to film a sick and dying
woman, but here she was,

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:41.730
in the living room, the corner
is where the TV used to be.

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.210
And the family was like, "Well,
why aren't you filming this?

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:45.450
Why aren't you taking pictures?"

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:50.450
So I did, and so what we see
here or we are going to see

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:57.050
is a little drama unfold

00:25:57.050 --> 00:26:01.680
that illustrates how the kin
display care for the dying

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.570
and apprentice ethical personhood.

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:06.740
And this emergence socialization routine

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:09.960
begins with Ba lying in
bed, in the living room,

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:11.800
her knees locked upright.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:15.020
Her daughter-in-law Lan
pleads with a comatose elder

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:16.363
to straighten these out.

00:26:18.170 --> 00:26:21.590
Ong who has come to watch
uses the non deferential,

00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:25.700
second personal pronoun me,
which only status seniors

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:29.590
use outstatus juniors
to establish hierarchy

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:33.580
as he calls out to Lan
until she looks up to him

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:37.910
and only to tell her
straighten out his wife's legs.

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:42.910
So I'll play this while talking
and then I'll play it again.

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:46.260
So with a smile Lan
answers in a plain voice,

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:47.410
she keeps grimacing.

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:50.270
She then adds this morning I
was able to stretch her a bit

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:52.200
but she's really struggling.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.560
Doesn't matter Ong reassures

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:56.810
but Lan continues with her complaint.

00:26:56.810 --> 00:27:00.800
Actually, she's mad at me,
I'm hurting her terribly.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:05.310
Approaching the bed Ong
mutters, down it hurts.

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:06.990
And then he gazes at as wife

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:10.410
as Lan continues to work on Ba's legs.

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:13.839
Bending over to clearly address
that this is what we see now

00:27:13.839 --> 00:27:16.767
Ong states in an authoritative
paternalistic voice,

00:27:16.767 --> 00:27:18.640
"Doesn't hurt."

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:22.637
Bending even closer, he exhorts
that in a scolding voice,

00:27:22.637 --> 00:27:24.560
"Endure the pain, stretch out the legs,

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:26.137
why stay cramped up?"

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:30.350
His eyes, then focusing on
Ba, he adds in a gentler,

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:33.350
quieter voice, raising
and lowering his chin

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:37.550
in a kind of entreaty,
"Stretch out, stretch out."

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:40.360
Throughout Lan continues to gently press

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:42.140
on her mother-in-law's legs

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:45.780
working to make that work
like with Ong's directive.

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:48.050
She overlaps with him protesting quietly,

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:50.690
I tried doing that while
Ong's sister-in-law

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:51.847
adds her own commands,

00:27:51.847 --> 00:27:55.000
"Stretch out the legs, stretch
your legs, lie on the bed."

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:59.393
And I can just do this again.

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:05.260
You all hear me okay?

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:06.490
My computer is kinda...

00:28:10.354 --> 00:28:12.771
(indistinct)

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:37.840
So adopting Goffman's
model of participant roles,

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:40.340
we see how the two generations organize

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:43.880
the interaction in gendered
and age graded ways.

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:47.050
It's the daughter-in-law Lan
who physically takes care

00:28:47.050 --> 00:28:48.810
of the ill woman there,

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:51.360
while the seniors direct her to keep doing

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:54.430
what she has in fact
been attempting to do.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:59.380
Lan in turn has little
recourse than to smile, comply,

00:28:59.380 --> 00:29:03.550
and only weakly protest that
she has been doing as told.

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:06.950
We learn even more about
responsibility, morality,

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:09.870
and power if we consider the ecology

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:11.120
of this microinteraction.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:15.020
This includes participants,
eye gaze, movements

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:18.429
and bodily use of space, which
reveal who is interpolated

00:29:18.429 --> 00:29:20.513
as a modified participant and how.

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:24.990
From this perspective, the
exchange looks less harsh,

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.790
it exemplifies act of care.

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:31.507
Despite Ba's grave condition,
her incompetent caregivers

00:29:31.507 --> 00:29:34.610
treat her as a ratified participant.

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:38.300
She's someone to whom they
attribute a will, emotions

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:39.390
and urgency.

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:42.820
Ong scolds, Lan pleads and
imagines are experiences

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:45.560
being scolded and in contrast

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:48.710
with those who are not
there daily to attend Ba,

00:29:48.710 --> 00:29:52.800
they at times address her
directly, not just as a mostly

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:55.410
but not yet dead body.

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:59.640
In shorter sense they enact
a we relationship with Ba.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:02.290
They treat her not as
assumed to be predecessor,

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:04.600
someone who shares
neither the same tenfold

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:07.590
nor spacial frame with
them, but as a consociate,

00:30:07.590 --> 00:30:10.340
someone with whom they could
still grow older together.

00:30:10.340 --> 00:30:12.710
And this often happens that
people treat each other

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:15.280
as consociates when their ancestors

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:17.050
are directly addressing them,

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:19.270
praying to them and feeding them.

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:21.380
They know that Ba cannot respond

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:23.890
either verbally or with her body.

00:30:23.890 --> 00:30:27.460
Yet Ong and Lan read her
nodded brow and stiff limbs

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:31.050
as expressions of pain
and stubborn reprove.

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:32.690
They treat her in other words,

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:35.620
as still endowed with
a rich cognitive life

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:39.360
even though she is motionless,
essentially unresponsive

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:41.883
and on the verge of death, they care.

00:30:42.956 --> 00:30:44.270
In this incipient narrative,

00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:48.730
Ong's exhortations to stretch
out, models for Lan how to act

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:51.780
in a difficult and
morally fraught instance

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:54.090
where the daughter-in-law
is afraid to hurt

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:57.170
and earn the wrath of her mother-in-law.

00:30:57.170 --> 00:31:00.660
In addressing Ba, Ong
indirectly exhorts Lan

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:03.530
to keep on tending to the old woman,

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:08.530
not to allow Ba's limbs to
stiffen and suffer even more.

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:10.990
This is an interpersonal form of care

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:14.160
that does not explicitly
implicate the market.

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:16.530
But though privately
done at home in passing,

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:21.290
it is a public act that demands
recognition and repetition,

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:22.123
and yet Lan and Ong,

00:31:22.123 --> 00:31:25.350
we're not professional
end of life caregivers,

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.770
they struggle to make Ba comfortable

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:29.663
and to manage their own discomfort.

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:33.523
Most days they did so in muted ways,

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:39.550
Lan smiling and Ong sleeping
in the face of Ba's calamity.

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:41.640
In the absence of prescribed rituals

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:44.840
which are in place for
tending and honoring the dead,

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:48.000
they worked instead at
treating with respect and care,

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:50.840
this matriarch, who
for so long had herself

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:53.193
managed the smooth
running of the household.

00:31:55.188 --> 00:31:57.430
They interpolated, not just each other

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:59.230
but also the comment that was there,

00:32:00.550 --> 00:32:04.040
as capable of moral action
as eminently competent

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:07.100
and yet imminently incompetent beings

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:10.670
who are engaged in an
ethical project of care

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:13.310
for and about one another.

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:16.500
And this is why the interaction
seems so fraught, I think.

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:19.130
We see unequally
positioned, family members

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:23.060
deliberate fleeting actions
over what the best good

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:26.620
and Sharon Maden Lee's term
is for the nearly dead.

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:28.620
They seem to act in unison,

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:31.610
in agreement that even though
stretching out Ba's legs

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:34.320
causes the inert but to recalcitrant body

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:36.820
and the person inhabiting it, pain

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:39.480
because she's still a living person

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:42.980
and they have to enjoin that
to endure the pain to kill

00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:46.060
and to act ethically in this world.

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:48.940
And yet this version of
care and ethical personhood

00:32:48.940 --> 00:32:52.760
was precarious, performed
daily in Ba's house

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:56.480
till her eventual death
the following spring,

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:58.690
it enlisted a range of Ba's relatives

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:02.980
who visited periodically
and spoke to and about her.

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:06.790
As they continued to participate
in the households rituals,

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:10.090
no one projected Ba into
the future as an ancestor,

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:12.800
and no one mentioned her imminent death.

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:15.490
They oriented her only as a present

00:33:16.937 --> 00:33:18.863
and suffering somewhat indignantly.

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:22.900
I could end by concluding
that these occasions

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:26.340
like Ba's earlier,
quiet endurance of pain,

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:28.820
where for months she did not seek care

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:32.640
for her high blood pressure
reflect local understandings

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:36.260
of gender virtuous care
and daily sacrifice

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:40.500
demanded by an economy
of retracting state care.

00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:42.253
But the story doesn't end here,

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:49.580
not everyone regarded Ong
and Ba as innocent victims,

00:33:49.580 --> 00:33:51.650
Ong and Ba's niece, An for example,

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:53.730
surprised me one December day

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:56.850
with a story of past
Mungs and transgressions.

00:33:56.850 --> 00:33:58.450
I briefly recount this story

00:33:58.450 --> 00:34:01.770
and the imminent critique
that Ba's current state

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:04.800
led An to project both
to show how protagonists

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:08.610
are not all equally burdened
with the ethical work

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:12.050
of enduring suffering for
the sake of another silently,

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:15.350
and also to show how
fragile love and care can be

00:34:15.350 --> 00:34:20.230
under both marketization
and socialism and under war.

00:34:20.230 --> 00:34:23.830
Decades earlier, Ong's brothers
fought on opposing sides

00:34:23.830 --> 00:34:25.183
of the American War.

00:34:26.141 --> 00:34:29.580
Any of the siblings
always seemed tight-knit,

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:32.600
they kept past divisions mute,

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:35.140
they like to recount
how Ong had done right

00:34:35.140 --> 00:34:37.720
by his youngest brother Sen, raising him

00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:39.320
and ensuring his education

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:42.810
since their father died young in 1948.

00:34:42.810 --> 00:34:46.100
These were compact,
coherent striving narratives

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:49.803
with clear beginnings and
morally predictable endings.

00:34:52.176 --> 00:34:55.730
But there is another
sideshadowing genre of narratives.

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:59.130
These stories highlight
ambiguity, ambivalence

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.120
and contradiction.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:05.110
Co-narrated and non-linear
they invite moral reflection

00:35:05.110 --> 00:35:06.690
and debate.

00:35:06.690 --> 00:35:09.510
In An's meandering story Ong and his wife

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:12.450
appear as withholding or suspending care

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:14.970
during and after the war.

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:18.350
An narrates this tale
of wrongs indirectly,

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:21.460
it's a sideshadowing
story told non-linearly

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:23.510
by weaving back and forth through time,

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:27.862
emphasizing moments of
connection, love and hurt.

00:35:27.862 --> 00:35:31.760
Ong and Ba had not
risked losing their house

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:35.140
for sheltering their American
collaborating brother Sen,

00:35:35.140 --> 00:35:38.300
nor had they helped his
faithful wife Ma visit him

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:40.720
in communist re-education
camp like Ong's sister

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:43.130
and An's mother Bi had done.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:46.220
Ong and Ba's passive
acts of omission of care,

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:49.360
which remained implicit and unnarrated,

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:53.640
also turned into sinful
commission when Ong sold off lands

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:56.440
that jointly belonged to all his siblings

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:58.693
and he left Sen's children nothing.

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:02.150
The threads of this story are tangled,

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:05.790
narrating shifting effective
stances, oblique connections

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:08.900
and disagreements that are not resolved,

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:12.593
and these are all worked
out in chapter four.

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:17.090
In sideshadowing fashion,

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:20.170
An presents a series of
contested moral stances

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:23.960
as protagonists hold conflicting
commitments to their kin.

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:27.673
She grieves for the now deceased Ma,

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:32.950
who died after the couple left Vietnam.

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:35.590
But when An was upset
at Sen, for remarrying,

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:39.360
her mother Bi defended him and scolded An,

00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:41.917
blood ties trump friendship.

00:36:41.917 --> 00:36:44.600
An's story continually
descends into the past

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:47.100
and navigates a presence seemingly blindly

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:50.870
without full clarity of hindsight.

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:53.820
It relies on silences and absences,

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:56.590
not saying that Ong had
not cared for his brother

00:36:56.590 --> 00:36:59.960
but pointing out how his sister had.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:03.460
She tells me, "siblings love,
they take care of each other,

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:05.850
they don't care about whatever happens,

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:07.880
they don't care about money."

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:10.100
And yet An's story is
filled with instances

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:11.160
in which gifts of money

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:14.350
stood for acts of love and refusal to give

00:37:14.350 --> 00:37:17.070
was equated with betrayal.

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:19.080
Contradiction pervades her stories

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:21.890
and still one point
emerges clearly at the end

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:24.773
when An's mother Bi,
takes over the telling.

00:37:25.740 --> 00:37:28.130
Land price is sold but Ong and his wife

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:29.520
barely shared profits

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:32.210
from the sale of their grandparents land

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:35.760
and refused her even
the smallest of loans.

00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:39.430
They didn't care, hearing
Bi and An use backshadowing

00:37:39.430 --> 00:37:43.570
using the present hindsight
to morally assess past deeds,

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:47.730
to link Ba's present state
to recent and past wrongs.

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:50.230
Their story which gained steam only slowly

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:53.290
suddenly crescendo to its conclusion.

00:37:53.290 --> 00:37:56.370
The present suffering
was karmic retribution

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:59.530
for a life that could
have been led otherwise.

00:37:59.530 --> 00:38:02.403
Her voice quietly furious Bi says,

00:38:03.989 --> 00:38:06.280
"I asked for so little
but he wouldn't give it.

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:09.680
This was recent before Ba Bay got sick

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:11.750
Ong said he doesn't have it, f***.

00:38:11.750 --> 00:38:14.267
We yield to him, whatever he wants."

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:18.040
Mother and daughter
abruptly stopped the account

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:20.570
with these unexpected curse.

00:38:20.570 --> 00:38:24.510
Resentments over care withheld
or inadequately performed

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:26.940
which could lie dormant for decades,

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:30.470
came to the surface with
Ba's present illness.

00:38:30.470 --> 00:38:34.310
This occasioned imminent
reflection over how to care

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:37.210
and imminent judgment
about who is competent

00:38:37.210 --> 00:38:39.523
or incompetent at doing so.

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:43.040
Tensions subtly expressed in instances

00:38:43.040 --> 00:38:45.720
such as when Ba's legs
need to be straightened

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:49.090
incite caregivers to
reflect on who has cared

00:38:49.090 --> 00:38:50.633
and who deserves caring.

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:54.070
A linguistic phenomenological lens

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:58.090
reveals how temporal horizons
reach into the past and future

00:38:58.090 --> 00:39:01.550
both in An and Bi's backshadowing
and sideshadowing stories

00:39:01.550 --> 00:39:05.320
that frame the past expectations
and promises unfulfilled

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:08.523
as foregone conclusions
or as unexpected turns.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:13.000
In Lan and Ong's incipient
and acted narratives,

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:15.800
ethical figures take
shape through complaints,

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:18.093
exhortations and silences.

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:22.940
Bi's stories and silences color
present concerns over care,

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:27.400
condense in Ba's figure lying
on her bed in the living room,

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:29.627
unconscious and unrestful.

00:39:30.802 --> 00:39:34.820
Bi is not only sustained
but also threaten to rupture

00:39:34.820 --> 00:39:38.980
shared understandings of who
is or was virtuous and why

00:39:38.980 --> 00:39:42.810
forcing and forging an
ethics not of cool judgment

00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:45.503
but life with cascading
ethic of love and regret.

00:39:45.503 --> 00:39:49.190
And so here, I'm talking
about the ways that ethics

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:51.820
isn't really about this
rational withdrawing

00:39:51.820 --> 00:39:54.320
and thinking about how to act,

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:59.320
it's also filled with emotion
in trying to behave morally.

00:40:01.660 --> 00:40:04.710
And yet still much more was kept at bay,

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:07.010
An and Bi continued to visit Ba,

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:10.310
effectively refusing to
jeopardize rituals of care

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:14.310
and dominant narratives of
virtue despite some tensions.

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:17.070
Their stories where
scornful language erupt

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:19.853
remain almost exclusively silenced.

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:23.980
I conclude then that by tracing
the unfolding emplotments

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:27.000
and intersections of Ong,
An's and Bi's stories

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:28.730
we can witness how precarious,

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:32.550
the world of enduring in
the name of love and care is

00:40:32.550 --> 00:40:35.850
and how quotidian sacrifices
remain for the most part,

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:39.693
silence in speech, but
demonstrated in action.

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:44.010
Competence and incompetence
are simultaneously immanent

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:47.350
and imminent and social
life bringing into relief

00:40:47.350 --> 00:40:50.610
the moral economy and
politics of care at home

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.100
where market constraints
form a mostly silenced

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.220
but not invisible template

00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:58.910
for what actions seem
possible and desirable.

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:01.930
Here, privatization is not an option,

00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:05.210
life is social and fraught to the core.

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:06.330
In the rest of the book,

00:41:06.330 --> 00:41:09.080
I continue to show how
participants navigate

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.480
these sorts of dilemmas and conflicts,

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:15.250
sometimes choosing
silence, other times gossip

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:17.280
to make claims of one another

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:19.953
in an ethically fraught relational world.

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:23.700
Again and again, we can
disown the cultural patterns

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:26.170
through attention to
the minutia of language

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:29.660
and interaction, which
color, and even constitute

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:30.960
the ways in which families

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:33.940
and communities struggle to cohere.

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:37.080
And these reveal yet again,
the old feminist insight,

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:39.240
that public ethics are to be found

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:42.533
in the after all not so
private domain of families,

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:47.290
families always already
political home lives.

00:41:47.290 --> 00:41:48.123
In the conclusion

00:41:48.123 --> 00:41:50.850
of the book "Mourning
in silent sacrifice",

00:41:50.850 --> 00:41:53.690
I revisit a piece I wrote
for "American Ethnologist"

00:41:53.690 --> 00:41:56.980
on the death and funeral
preparations in another family

00:41:56.980 --> 00:41:58.680
where we see how the bereaved

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:01.690
attempt to contain their grief.

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:05.630
Here too, I argue that
repetition, like change,

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:08.260
doesn't always beget futurity or natality

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:11.350
in Hannah Ahmad's terms,
even in Vietnamese,

00:42:11.350 --> 00:42:14.670
where he sends signals life
and suffering in silence

00:42:14.670 --> 00:42:16.110
for the sake of love.

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:16.943
Thank you.

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:23.467
So I'm gonna stop the share
to open it up to discussion

00:42:26.848 --> 00:42:27.681
and questions.

00:42:27.681 --> 00:42:29.100
- Thank you, Merav.

00:42:29.100 --> 00:42:33.108
So everyone we have, well beyond the hour

00:42:33.108 --> 00:42:36.330
to engage in question and answer.

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:40.160
You can raise your hand
using the hand raise function

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:43.600
or send a note to the chat
and I'll keep a running list

00:42:43.600 --> 00:42:47.580
and then allow Merav to answer
the questions as they come.

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:52.440
So Oscar, did I see your hand go up?

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:53.273
Go ahead.

00:42:56.540 --> 00:42:58.230
- Thanks very much Merav,

00:42:58.230 --> 00:43:01.843
it's a fascinating story that you told,

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:07.520
really I liked it the stories
but then also the actions,

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:15.063
the silencing as well.

00:43:16.757 --> 00:43:21.610
And so it reminds me a
bit of, how to put this?

00:43:24.052 --> 00:43:27.480
There was once this Medieval European

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:32.480
or relations European
philosopher, Erasmus Rotterdam.

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:34.000
One of the things that he wrote

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:36.980
was etiquette books, "How to Behave".

00:43:36.980 --> 00:43:40.170
And if you would read
these etiquette books now,

00:43:40.170 --> 00:43:42.730
it's about don't spit on the floor,

00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:46.140
don't relieve yourself
in the corner, et cetera.

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:49.840
Now these things would not be necessary

00:43:49.840 --> 00:43:53.940
in most places to remind people of

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:58.367
and (indistinct) Rotterdam,
he was not the only one

00:43:58.367 --> 00:44:00.667
who wrote it, it was
like a genre at the time.

00:44:01.525 --> 00:44:06.525
(indistinct), on the one
hand, there is an emphasis

00:44:09.459 --> 00:44:12.724
about doing something or not
doing something (indistinct),

00:44:12.724 --> 00:44:15.600
there's a reality where
this is a bit different.

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:18.460
And your story really reminded me

00:44:23.094 --> 00:44:26.243
what people say and like
be completely different

00:44:28.570 --> 00:44:31.333
from the (indistinct)

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:45.900
has heard that, (indistinct),

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:50.730
you hear that like a million times, right?

00:44:52.580 --> 00:44:57.580
But I'm wondering, could
you reflect on that?

00:44:58.774 --> 00:45:00.837
Because I have the deep
suspicion (indistinct),

00:45:02.034 --> 00:45:06.633
but masks very often a lack of it,

00:45:08.524 --> 00:45:12.570
in the personal relationships,
but reflections about that.

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:16.890
- Yeah, I think I heard the question,

00:45:16.890 --> 00:45:21.890
it was very mummed but
I think if the question

00:45:22.230 --> 00:45:25.200
that people are constantly
exhorting each other

00:45:25.200 --> 00:45:29.950
to perform tinh cam and
absolutely what I saw

00:45:29.950 --> 00:45:31.450
with tinh cam is that everybody

00:45:31.450 --> 00:45:34.430
is in some ways striving
to do it but in fact,

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:38.760
it's oftentimes weaponized
to use against people.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:40.597
So you don't say to people,

00:45:40.597 --> 00:45:43.440
"Oh you didn't sacrifice enough."

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:45.670
Or, "I sacrificed so much for you

00:45:45.670 --> 00:45:47.650
why aren't you doing this for me?"

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.530
But the way that they use tinh cam,

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:52.860
even though it's supposed to
have a positive connotation

00:45:52.860 --> 00:45:55.530
is always, well, this
person isn't living up

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:58.610
to this standard, you don't show tinh cam,

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:02.200
and that's a lot of what I
show also in chapter five

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:05.260
is the ways that people
gossip about one another

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:08.490
and how it's oftentimes women
gossiping about each other

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:10.750
while kinda letting men off the hook

00:46:11.750 --> 00:46:16.750
in expressing how men are
so virtuous for helping

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:21.040
or doing this and that
worshiping, being convivial,

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:24.720
and a lot of times criticizing each other

00:46:25.773 --> 00:46:27.333
for not doing enough.

00:46:28.314 --> 00:46:31.450
And so really these
principles, they're idealized

00:46:31.450 --> 00:46:34.850
and it's very easy to use
a structuralist argument

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:36.560
and say, "Well, this contains life."

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:41.560
But in fact that's really
ethnographically dishonest

00:46:41.620 --> 00:46:46.563
to say that these principles
guide every single behavior.

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:51.700
People are oftentimes in
conflict with one another,

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:54.060
and part of it also has
to do with this kind of,

00:46:54.060 --> 00:46:55.880
it's not like it's a straight hierarchy

00:46:55.880 --> 00:46:59.950
where one dimension encompasses the other,

00:46:59.950 --> 00:47:03.490
they intersect and they
rub up against each other.

00:47:03.490 --> 00:47:05.580
And this is where tinh cam works

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:08.480
as a way of disciplining people

00:47:08.480 --> 00:47:13.480
not just as a way of acting
well or being good and virtuous.

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:17.670
Did I answer that?

00:47:20.780 --> 00:47:25.113
- You did, I apologize for
the bad quality of sound.

00:47:26.830 --> 00:47:29.260
- Thank you, Merav and
thanks Oscar for the question

00:47:29.260 --> 00:47:31.463
and we have a question from David Lindsay.

00:47:37.250 --> 00:47:38.660
- Unmute.

00:47:38.660 --> 00:47:39.923
Yes, thank you for this,

00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:43.730
I've never seen anything quite like that

00:47:43.730 --> 00:47:48.730
and it was fascinating,
and it was surprising

00:47:49.390 --> 00:47:52.370
how it kept getting deeper and deeper,

00:47:52.370 --> 00:47:53.890
I was quite impressed.

00:47:53.890 --> 00:47:55.231
I wanna go back to something

00:47:55.231 --> 00:47:58.750
in the beginning of your presentation

00:47:58.750 --> 00:48:02.570
you talked about asymmetrical reciprocity,

00:48:02.570 --> 00:48:06.080
which is something I think I know about

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:11.040
but then you said it's
respect above and yield below.

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:13.823
And I want you to explain
what yield below means.

00:48:15.440 --> 00:48:20.440
- Yeah, so essentially this
notion of respect above yield

00:48:21.290 --> 00:48:26.290
to those below means that
if you have the means,

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:28.410
if you're a senior, your job,

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:32.137
isn't just to get all the
benefits, to get the respect,

00:48:32.137 --> 00:48:37.137
it's also to give, to
yield to those below you

00:48:38.465 --> 00:48:40.223
to take care of them really.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:45.810
So in some ways, for the China scholars,

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:47.283
it's a bit like the guanxi,

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:51.500
sorry if I'm mispronouncing
that, but it's really

00:48:52.467 --> 00:48:56.970
about that you are beneficent
and kind of paternalistic

00:48:56.970 --> 00:48:58.910
to those who have less than you,

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:00.740
to those who are junior to you.

00:49:00.740 --> 00:49:05.740
And this is where we do have reciprocity

00:49:06.670 --> 00:49:10.400
and the sense that each side
does things for each other

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:12.760
but this is on a much
longer temporal scale.

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:15.580
And so if parents and grandparents

00:49:15.580 --> 00:49:18.060
are taking care of kids, for example,

00:49:18.060 --> 00:49:18.990
they're yielding to them.

00:49:18.990 --> 00:49:21.550
We see it even with a greeting routine

00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:25.163
where the mother corrects
the little baby's posture.

00:49:26.460 --> 00:49:30.110
She does it in some ways that
physically she pulls her up

00:49:30.110 --> 00:49:34.910
and it's a little bit
harsh, but then the words

00:49:34.910 --> 00:49:37.560
and the gestures are also gentle.

00:49:37.560 --> 00:49:40.990
And this is how people
I think get socialized

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:45.990
into this idea that you
don't give begrudgingly,

00:49:46.340 --> 00:49:47.703
you give willingly.

00:49:48.570 --> 00:49:52.210
And when you don't people criticize you

00:49:52.210 --> 00:49:57.083
for not really sacrificing or
not really enacting tinh cam.

00:49:57.930 --> 00:50:02.180
And so when families put in so much effort

00:50:02.180 --> 00:50:04.210
for the ancestors,

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:07.540
it's ostensibly not to
make the ancestors angry.

00:50:07.540 --> 00:50:11.270
And it's also to show
each other that they care,

00:50:11.270 --> 00:50:13.590
and that they're putting
up these resources

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:15.620
and they're feeding one another.

00:50:15.620 --> 00:50:19.560
They're feeding those who
need also in times of want

00:50:19.560 --> 00:50:21.330
but there is also the expectation

00:50:21.330 --> 00:50:23.393
that the ancestor always give to them.

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:26.160
That they don't punish them,

00:50:26.160 --> 00:50:28.870
and moreover, they give them fortune

00:50:28.870 --> 00:50:31.480
that you're able to succeed in life.

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:33.670
And so in some ways that's what I mean

00:50:33.670 --> 00:50:38.060
by yielding below that it's
not just the upwards directed

00:50:38.060 --> 00:50:41.263
but that it's bi-directional,
but not equal.

00:50:42.320 --> 00:50:43.750
- Thank you very much.

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:48.637
It's so interesting how
these very Vietnamese affects

00:50:50.110 --> 00:50:52.173
are in some ways universal.

00:50:54.450 --> 00:50:55.610
- Thank you, David.

00:50:55.610 --> 00:50:59.630
And Merav there's a
question from Huetam Tai

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:01.133
in the chat.

00:51:03.514 --> 00:51:05.610
Huetam, would you like
to ask your question

00:51:05.610 --> 00:51:08.480
or did you want us just
to read it from the chat?

00:51:08.480 --> 00:51:09.963
- [Huetam] No, I can ask.

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:19.750
I've had encounters with
Vietnamese women throughout,

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:22.430
actually Central Vietnam.

00:51:22.430 --> 00:51:27.430
I was escorting American
teachers and every single guide

00:51:28.320 --> 00:51:32.677
talked about how difficult it was to deal

00:51:34.750 --> 00:51:38.550
with their obligations
as daughters-in-law.

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:43.090
They were living with the parents-in-law

00:51:43.090 --> 00:51:47.780
and they were expected to
care for the mothers-in-law

00:51:47.780 --> 00:51:52.780
and sisters-in-law and felt
that it was a real burden,

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:56.293
both physically and emotionally.

00:51:57.863 --> 00:52:02.863
So I'm just wondering about
the gender of your informants,

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:08.730
were they mostly women, did you talk a lot

00:52:08.730 --> 00:52:13.730
to the men in their families
and was there a difference

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:18.960
in the way they approach
obligations towards parents

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:22.570
or did the men expect that
their wives would take care

00:52:22.570 --> 00:52:25.380
of their own parents?

00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:30.380
And in the United States
most caregivers are female,

00:52:33.220 --> 00:52:38.220
maybe two thirds, three
quarters of caregivers are,

00:52:39.490 --> 00:52:44.130
including people who have experience

00:52:44.130 --> 00:52:48.883
dealing with things like physical therapy,

00:52:49.901 --> 00:52:54.690
I've had experience with
visiting nurses, so I know.

00:52:55.840 --> 00:53:00.840
And finally, in a lot of my experiences

00:53:01.050 --> 00:53:05.923
women are the ones who
make decisions as to money,

00:53:06.770 --> 00:53:09.050
how much they can spend,

00:53:09.050 --> 00:53:14.050
how much they can lend out and so on.

00:53:15.260 --> 00:53:18.893
So I was wondering again,

00:53:19.810 --> 00:53:24.810
when it comes to this
asymmetric relationship,

00:53:27.690 --> 00:53:32.610
do women bear most of the burden and why?

00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:37.133
Is that something that
is culturally dictated

00:53:41.360 --> 00:53:46.360
or specific to the families
that you interviewed?

00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:53.470
- I wanna say that yes, as a woman,

00:53:53.470 --> 00:53:57.490
I probably interacted
more with women than men

00:53:57.490 --> 00:54:02.490
but I didn't exclusively interact
with women also with men.

00:54:04.030 --> 00:54:06.600
And what I found is that that saying

00:54:06.600 --> 00:54:10.670
about women being (speaks in
foreign language), is true,

00:54:10.670 --> 00:54:14.750
oftentimes women also earned
more than their husbands

00:54:16.348 --> 00:54:18.760
and that could create some conflict.

00:54:18.760 --> 00:54:23.760
Although oftentimes I found
that men kinda took it anyway.

00:54:25.552 --> 00:54:30.552
And women do provide a
lot of the caregiving

00:54:31.220 --> 00:54:36.200
and do a disproportionate
burden of the cooking,

00:54:36.200 --> 00:54:38.983
the cleaning, the tending to kids.

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:45.600
I am a bit reluctant to
make universalist claims,

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:49.850
I think Sherry Ortner does a very good job

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:54.850
of explaining this
culturally, back in the '70s,

00:54:55.280 --> 00:54:58.130
as female to male, as
a nature is to culture,

00:54:58.130 --> 00:55:03.130
but I think that we can also
find critiques of those.

00:55:03.280 --> 00:55:08.280
But I found that surprisingly,
some of the women

00:55:11.070 --> 00:55:16.070
actually embraced being
a good daughter-in-law.

00:55:16.270 --> 00:55:18.830
In chapter five, I actually have a case

00:55:18.830 --> 00:55:22.720
that's a little bit
different where the wife

00:55:22.720 --> 00:55:27.720
uses her earning power to
distance herself from the in-laws.

00:55:28.580 --> 00:55:30.673
And of course she gets criticized for it,

00:55:31.720 --> 00:55:35.520
but she also appreciates the ways

00:55:35.520 --> 00:55:37.900
that her husband is extremely filial.

00:55:37.900 --> 00:55:42.360
And so we end up having
the husbands sisters

00:55:42.360 --> 00:55:45.080
and the wife praising
him for being so virtuous

00:55:45.080 --> 00:55:49.090
while they're doing a
lot of the work of care

00:55:49.090 --> 00:55:51.203
and conviviality.

00:55:53.720 --> 00:55:56.530
And then I also had some families, rare,

00:55:56.530 --> 00:56:01.200
but it happens sometimes where
the husband actually lived

00:56:01.200 --> 00:56:03.420
with the parents-in-law.

00:56:03.420 --> 00:56:08.210
And so yes, women take on more

00:56:08.210 --> 00:56:11.750
and I think part of it is that association

00:56:14.954 --> 00:56:17.910
of sacrifice being the virtue of women,

00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:21.490
that from the very beginning hy sinh

00:56:21.490 --> 00:56:24.903
is connected also with
birth, with giving birth.

00:56:25.830 --> 00:56:29.000
And so it's a kind of dual-edged discourse

00:56:29.000 --> 00:56:33.790
where the expectation that you be moral,

00:56:33.790 --> 00:56:37.930
that you give willingly,
that you take care,

00:56:37.930 --> 00:56:42.930
that you be virtuous, is
obviously also disciplining

00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:47.623
and can be experienced
as hurtful and violent.

00:56:48.770 --> 00:56:53.660
And so in that sense,
I think that obviously,

00:56:53.660 --> 00:56:55.440
you have so much experience

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:57.650
and I wouldn't discount it at all.

00:56:57.650 --> 00:57:01.840
It's very much the case
that women and I would say,

00:57:01.840 --> 00:57:03.890
especially it's the poor women

00:57:05.284 --> 00:57:09.173
who end up shouldering
a lot of this burden.

00:57:10.210 --> 00:57:11.370
- Thank you.

00:57:11.370 --> 00:57:14.360
We have a question from Diu-Huong,

00:57:14.360 --> 00:57:15.930
Diu-Huong please go ahead,

00:57:15.930 --> 00:57:16.763
- [Diu-Huong] Hi every one,

00:57:16.763 --> 00:57:19.340
I'm so sorry that my Internet is unstable

00:57:19.340 --> 00:57:21.753
so I can't turn on my camera.

00:57:22.740 --> 00:57:26.280
So thank you for your talk,
it's really interesting

00:57:26.280 --> 00:57:28.320
and I'm so glad to see another research

00:57:28.320 --> 00:57:29.970
on the central region of Vietnam.

00:57:30.820 --> 00:57:33.270
So I'm just curious though

00:57:33.270 --> 00:57:35.883
because when we talk
about Quang Nam people,

00:57:37.552 --> 00:57:40.900
Vietnamese people this saying,
(speaks in foreign language)

00:57:40.900 --> 00:57:44.980
which is the people from
Vietnam, like to talk back.

00:57:44.980 --> 00:57:48.730
And I'm just curious if
your research looked at that

00:57:48.730 --> 00:57:53.440
kind of specific characteristics
of the Quang Nam people

00:57:53.440 --> 00:57:58.007
and whether those would
affect any of the conception

00:57:59.440 --> 00:58:04.440
of hy sinh and tinh cam and
if that change over time,

00:58:05.330 --> 00:58:10.330
or if you saw similar
things in Quang Nam, Da Nang

00:58:11.487 --> 00:58:16.487
and other location if
you pay attention to that

00:58:18.936 --> 00:58:20.679
in other locations as well.

00:58:20.679 --> 00:58:21.799
Thank you.

00:58:21.799 --> 00:58:23.240
- Thank you.

00:58:23.240 --> 00:58:26.090
Yeah, I mean, the majority of research

00:58:26.090 --> 00:58:28.383
was in Da Nang and Quang Nam,

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:34.370
so I had much less observations in Hanoi

00:58:34.370 --> 00:58:38.053
some a bit in Hoi and
Saigon, Ho Chi Minh City.

00:58:39.110 --> 00:58:40.280
Do people talk back?

00:58:40.280 --> 00:58:42.780
Of course they do sometimes.

00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:47.730
I mean, we're all humans,
but I would say that...

00:58:47.730 --> 00:58:52.730
Again, I presented a
framework of how to behave

00:58:52.850 --> 00:58:56.550
that people strive to
or criticize each other

00:58:56.550 --> 00:58:59.623
for not living up to it sometimes.

00:59:00.620 --> 00:59:04.220
But for the most part,
there is that kind of effort

00:59:04.220 --> 00:59:09.220
to hy sinh and tinh cam,
even as people yeah,

00:59:09.830 --> 00:59:10.990
they talk back.

00:59:10.990 --> 00:59:13.290
Yeah, they joke about each other,

00:59:13.290 --> 00:59:16.233
yeah they tease, sometimes they hurt.

00:59:18.310 --> 00:59:21.090
And so all of it, I don't wanna present

00:59:21.090 --> 00:59:25.307
a picture perfect kind
of idea of this stability

00:59:28.490 --> 00:59:33.030
that people are solid into their roles,

00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:35.200
but rather it's that dynamism

00:59:35.200 --> 00:59:38.630
in how they interact with one another

00:59:38.630 --> 00:59:39.810
that makes it interesting.

00:59:39.810 --> 00:59:44.322
And that's why I find
that attention to language

00:59:44.322 --> 00:59:47.650
and particularly the
kind of the microdynamics

00:59:47.650 --> 00:59:50.480
of interaction that
helps us see the extent

00:59:50.480 --> 00:59:55.480
to which they do or don't
violate certain cultural norms.

00:59:56.660 --> 00:59:57.820
Does that make sense

00:59:57.820 --> 01:00:00.483
or did I completely
misunderstand your question?

01:00:01.470 --> 01:00:02.935
- [Diu-Huong] Yeah,
actually I was thinking

01:00:02.935 --> 01:00:07.163
more like the region differences?

01:00:09.009 --> 01:00:11.693
- I think that's a great question,

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:17.240
and I feel like having spent
the majority of my time

01:00:17.240 --> 01:00:22.240
in Central Vietnam, I
don't think I am the one

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:25.210
best qualified to talk about the North.

01:00:25.210 --> 01:00:28.130
I'd say in the North is
actually one of the places

01:00:28.130 --> 01:00:30.960
where I heard women
more explicitly complain

01:00:30.960 --> 01:00:35.260
about these expectations
that they end up taking on

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:39.910
the role of virtuous wives

01:00:39.910 --> 01:00:43.593
at the same time that they
have to do everything else.

01:00:44.490 --> 01:00:47.610
But again, I think we have
many people in the audience

01:00:47.610 --> 01:00:49.660
that I've done much more
research in the North

01:00:49.660 --> 01:00:51.720
that can tell us more about that.

01:00:51.720 --> 01:00:53.100
And I would need to go back

01:00:53.100 --> 01:00:57.300
and spend significantly
more time in other regions

01:00:57.300 --> 01:00:58.833
to make a claim about that.

01:01:01.540 --> 01:01:02.530
- Thanks, Merav.

01:01:02.530 --> 01:01:04.623
Next question from Qui Ha Nguyen.

01:01:06.380 --> 01:01:07.920
- Thank you Merav,

01:01:07.920 --> 01:01:12.310
for your fascinating
presentation on the topic.

01:01:12.310 --> 01:01:14.520
I'm very interested in the...

01:01:14.520 --> 01:01:17.810
Actually my research on
Vietnamese revolutionary cinema

01:01:17.810 --> 01:01:22.140
focus on the ideas of
womanhood and motherhood

01:01:22.140 --> 01:01:27.140
which somehow share or
overlap in the sense of care,

01:01:28.323 --> 01:01:31.270
that is why sacrifice,

01:01:31.270 --> 01:01:36.270
which your fascinating research present.

01:01:36.480 --> 01:01:38.700
I have a two very brief questions.

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:41.913
The first one is about
the idea of silence.

01:01:43.028 --> 01:01:45.770
You talk about silence
in the sense of social

01:01:45.770 --> 01:01:48.043
and community presence.

01:01:49.424 --> 01:01:54.424
And this link to the point that Diu-Huong

01:01:54.460 --> 01:01:59.460
had just presented, I'll pose
it, it is about the locality.

01:02:02.060 --> 01:02:04.770
So for that, I would like to ask you

01:02:04.770 --> 01:02:09.770
about did you consider
the historical dimension

01:02:11.920 --> 01:02:13.910
of the ideas of silence?

01:02:13.910 --> 01:02:16.780
Because Quang Nam and Da
Nang, all Central Vietnam,

01:02:16.780 --> 01:02:19.590
I'm from Quang Ngai and
then my whole family

01:02:19.590 --> 01:02:22.700
and then the neighbor, on the
one hand they share openly

01:02:22.700 --> 01:02:26.940
but on as many, many points,
they just keep that for them.

01:02:26.940 --> 01:02:30.850
They just don't want
to expose all the pain

01:02:30.850 --> 01:02:34.201
that they don't know if it works

01:02:34.201 --> 01:02:38.360
for a young generation like me

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:41.650
to share which point they gonna
open up but at some point.

01:02:41.650 --> 01:02:43.490
So I would like to ask about the silence

01:02:43.490 --> 01:02:44.840
which is linked to the ideas

01:02:44.840 --> 01:02:48.250
of historical dimension and
that's particularly linked

01:02:48.250 --> 01:02:53.250
to the locality that you
use at Quang Nam and Da Nang

01:02:55.560 --> 01:02:59.720
as the place to do your research.

01:02:59.720 --> 01:03:03.553
The second question, it is
about the ideas of tinh cam,

01:03:04.883 --> 01:03:09.883
and I see that you talk,
and then your research

01:03:11.240 --> 01:03:15.820
focus on the ideas of material relation

01:03:15.820 --> 01:03:20.820
and is this really amazing
especially you show the clips

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:24.880
where the daughter-in-law
and the whole family

01:03:24.880 --> 01:03:27.520
have that kind of interaction.

01:03:27.520 --> 01:03:28.950
I have another question,

01:03:28.950 --> 01:03:33.950
a question that keep coming up to me

01:03:35.720 --> 01:03:38.030
when I was listening to your presentation

01:03:38.030 --> 01:03:43.030
is nowadays many people
like Vietnam, they migrate

01:03:45.090 --> 01:03:48.940
to Ho Chi Minh City, Hanoi or even abroad

01:03:48.940 --> 01:03:52.520
to earn for living.

01:03:52.520 --> 01:03:56.420
And that kind of care, material care,

01:03:56.420 --> 01:03:59.520
on the one hand is still as you show,

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:03.220
but on the other hand, they
show other form of care,

01:04:03.220 --> 01:04:07.770
like they send remittance,
or they do FaceTime chat

01:04:07.770 --> 01:04:11.870
every time, like for an hour
or two, similar like that.

01:04:11.870 --> 01:04:16.870
And then if your research
actually touch on that

01:04:18.497 --> 01:04:22.840
or any other form of care
rather than the material care

01:04:22.840 --> 01:04:26.380
that you presented, wonderful already.

01:04:26.380 --> 01:04:28.016
Thank you so much.

01:04:28.016 --> 01:04:29.550
- Thank you.

01:04:29.550 --> 01:04:31.963
Yeah, so with tinh cam, absolutely.

01:04:32.959 --> 01:04:35.420
I mean, of course a lot
of it, the most obvious

01:04:35.420 --> 01:04:37.690
is the cooking, the cleaning, the tending,

01:04:37.690 --> 01:04:41.800
but it is also like
you said the going away

01:04:41.800 --> 01:04:44.020
and earning money and sending it back.

01:04:44.020 --> 01:04:49.020
And there was so much when
I talk to Vietnamese people

01:04:50.450 --> 01:04:52.650
my experience has been from everywhere

01:04:52.650 --> 01:04:55.490
but I might be wrong is oftentimes

01:04:55.490 --> 01:05:00.320
talking about their mothers
and their parents, hy sinh,

01:05:00.320 --> 01:05:05.320
the ways that these
relationships of care continue

01:05:05.660 --> 01:05:10.110
and span distances and span generations.

01:05:10.110 --> 01:05:12.720
And the same with
silence, I mean of course,

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:15.240
there was the sonic
silence of staying quiet

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:19.913
but there was also that silence
surrounding painful events.

01:05:20.960 --> 01:05:25.940
And one of the things that
struck me is, for example,

01:05:25.940 --> 01:05:29.210
when somebody died, once they were buried,

01:05:29.210 --> 01:05:33.218
you were supposed to hold your feelings in

01:05:33.218 --> 01:05:35.260
and not talk about it.

01:05:35.260 --> 01:05:38.040
And I think a similar attitude

01:05:38.040 --> 01:05:40.240
about kind of that painful past,

01:05:40.240 --> 01:05:43.540
the historical suffering of war

01:05:43.540 --> 01:05:48.540
was not something that people
generally opened up about.

01:05:51.090 --> 01:05:56.090
I have come to wonder
when these things happen,

01:05:57.940 --> 01:06:00.570
like at what point generationally.

01:06:00.570 --> 01:06:03.740
And I guess I think of
it because my grandmother

01:06:06.170 --> 01:06:08.660
was a Holocaust survivor
and she never, ever, ever

01:06:08.660 --> 01:06:11.340
spoke about it to us, to the family.

01:06:11.340 --> 01:06:16.270
And my mom did not think of her mom much,

01:06:16.270 --> 01:06:18.763
like a Holocaust survivor
except sometimes, yes.

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:23.250
But then, my generation is asking about it

01:06:23.250 --> 01:06:25.400
and wanting to find out.

01:06:25.400 --> 01:06:30.400
And I wonder if in Vietnam
also it's that third generation

01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:33.580
and beyond that are asking the questions

01:06:33.580 --> 01:06:38.580
that are wanting to hear, and
that maybe as people get older

01:06:39.130 --> 01:06:43.710
those who did survive
atrocities and hardship,

01:06:43.710 --> 01:06:48.710
but maybe they become more
ready to speak and to open up.

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:52.110
And that one, I think it
just requires more time,

01:06:52.110 --> 01:06:54.380
and so maybe when I was there

01:06:54.380 --> 01:06:57.790
it wasn't so much spoken
about, but that doesn't mean

01:06:57.790 --> 01:07:01.090
that there is gonna be
silence and perpetuity.

01:07:01.090 --> 01:07:04.833
And so definitely, I think
that's a great question.

01:07:06.115 --> 01:07:07.450
And same with tinh cam,

01:07:07.450 --> 01:07:10.050
I mean, it continues in different ways.

01:07:10.050 --> 01:07:13.450
It's just that the point
is that it's not like love

01:07:13.450 --> 01:07:17.000
isn't just about feelings,
it's also about doings

01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:20.400
and you don't have to be right there to do

01:07:20.400 --> 01:07:23.280
but that you show through
actions, not just move one word.

01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:24.950
So if you say more, I love
you, I love you, I love you,

01:07:24.950 --> 01:07:28.010
well, yeah, that's good
but what can you do for me

01:07:28.010 --> 01:07:30.900
is the bigger question often.

01:07:30.900 --> 01:07:32.090
That makes sense?

01:07:32.090 --> 01:07:32.923
- Thank you.

01:07:34.870 --> 01:07:38.130
- Thanks, we have a
question from Loni Jones.

01:07:38.130 --> 01:07:39.820
Loni, did you wanna ask it yourself

01:07:39.820 --> 01:07:41.760
or did you want me to
read it from the chat?

01:07:41.760 --> 01:07:42.593
- Sure I can ask.

01:07:42.593 --> 01:07:47.470
I'm just thinking about the
purpose of sacrifice and care

01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:52.493
more generally not specifically
in this context, I guess.

01:07:55.729 --> 01:07:57.480
And I got hung up when
you said the word burden

01:07:57.480 --> 01:07:58.933
and I'm just thinking about,

01:08:01.390 --> 01:08:04.220
you said there were some
women who did sort of relish

01:08:04.220 --> 01:08:07.220
in this work, did you see that very often?

01:08:07.220 --> 01:08:11.173
And do you think that's like
a lesser way of being as your,

01:08:12.410 --> 01:08:17.200
I guess your focus is kind
of on this unequal dynamics

01:08:18.060 --> 01:08:18.913
that you see?

01:08:20.160 --> 01:08:21.840
- Yeah, no, that's a great question.

01:08:21.840 --> 01:08:25.320
And thank you for calling
me on it because in fact,

01:08:25.320 --> 01:08:28.060
it's almost unfair of
me to say burden, right?

01:08:28.060 --> 01:08:30.760
Because a lot of women actually, yeah,

01:08:30.760 --> 01:08:33.290
they said, "Yeah, my husband could clean,

01:08:33.290 --> 01:08:35.020
could cook, and all
that, but I wanna do it,

01:08:35.020 --> 01:08:37.690
I wouldn't be a proper
woman if I didn't do it."

01:08:37.690 --> 01:08:42.510
And so I think that that
is kind of my imposition

01:08:42.510 --> 01:08:46.410
of looking from the outside and
saying, oh, that's a burden.

01:08:46.410 --> 01:08:50.610
But from many women,
they do also internalize

01:08:50.610 --> 01:08:55.033
and cherish that work, not
always, not all the time,

01:08:57.295 --> 01:09:01.390
and that's where if there
is that kind of tension

01:09:01.390 --> 01:09:02.697
between a discourse that says,

01:09:02.697 --> 01:09:05.020
"Well this is my job, and
this is what I want to do.

01:09:05.020 --> 01:09:07.150
And I'm doing it willingly because I love

01:09:07.150 --> 01:09:11.660
and because I care and then
sometimes it rubs up against

01:09:11.660 --> 01:09:14.300
but I could also be doing other things."

01:09:14.300 --> 01:09:19.300
So I think it's absolutely
right of you to question that.

01:09:19.410 --> 01:09:20.370
- Yeah, I'm just thinking

01:09:20.370 --> 01:09:22.560
about what is the alternative thing to do?

01:09:22.560 --> 01:09:27.390
I mean, I guess the unequal
part of it is I guess,

01:09:27.390 --> 01:09:30.710
distressing but like what
else is more important

01:09:30.710 --> 01:09:32.030
than to care for?

01:09:32.030 --> 01:09:33.920
- I mean, but that's the thing, right?

01:09:33.920 --> 01:09:37.890
That there is a kind of
sense that in an ideal case

01:09:37.890 --> 01:09:42.890
it works out well that you
give some and you get some

01:09:43.500 --> 01:09:45.410
and you're not giving the same.

01:09:45.410 --> 01:09:48.530
And this is why these acts of
care are not fungible, right?

01:09:48.530 --> 01:09:51.610
It's not that I wash your
dishes and you wash my dishes

01:09:51.610 --> 01:09:53.670
and where even the idea is

01:09:53.670 --> 01:09:56.290
that there is more of
that temporal horizon

01:09:56.290 --> 01:09:59.060
that we are constantly in a relationship,

01:09:59.060 --> 01:09:59.893
and from creating that (indistinct).

01:09:59.893 --> 01:10:02.522
- Creates a relationship, yeah.

01:10:02.522 --> 01:10:04.910
- Yeah, through that inequality.

01:10:04.910 --> 01:10:06.373
- Really beautiful work.

01:10:09.410 --> 01:10:12.480
- Thank you for the great
question and the answer.

01:10:12.480 --> 01:10:14.433
Our final question will be from Thuy,

01:10:15.840 --> 01:10:19.480
and then what I'll do after
we've answered this question,

01:10:19.480 --> 01:10:22.290
I'll stop the recording
and then we'll keep it open

01:10:22.290 --> 01:10:25.050
for informal chitchat for
those who would like to stay

01:10:25.050 --> 01:10:26.460
another 15 minutes after that.

01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:27.620
So go ahead Thuy.

01:10:28.871 --> 01:10:30.460
- Okay, thank you.

01:10:30.460 --> 01:10:34.620
It's interesting, a very interesting book.

01:10:34.620 --> 01:10:38.890
I can see like my whole
family and my other family,

01:10:38.890 --> 01:10:40.853
Vietnamese family in your book,

01:10:41.780 --> 01:10:44.193
like the condition and how people care.

01:10:46.180 --> 01:10:48.453
I have some questions.

01:10:49.340 --> 01:10:52.750
The part it says in some
way in your presentation,

01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:55.600
you talk about discourses,

01:10:55.600 --> 01:10:58.420
and I'm not sure what you
mean by that discourses,

01:10:58.420 --> 01:11:03.240
in gender equality or if you have time

01:11:03.240 --> 01:11:06.870
to talk more about that when
we are in informal chat.

01:11:06.870 --> 01:11:10.260
But another questions is about behavior

01:11:10.260 --> 01:11:15.260
and attitudes of the
women like where the...

01:11:15.860 --> 01:11:19.520
Because I see in the
video and the pictures.

01:11:19.520 --> 01:11:24.500
so much of the women, at
least somehow household women

01:11:24.500 --> 01:11:29.500
like staying at home, doing
some not professional jobs.

01:11:29.570 --> 01:11:34.570
So whether education and
jobs and economic condition

01:11:35.450 --> 01:11:39.960
of the family gender behavior
and attitudes of the women

01:11:39.960 --> 01:11:43.760
of the people in term
of care and sacrificing.

01:11:43.760 --> 01:11:48.760
Because I see it in my
family and the family

01:11:48.800 --> 01:11:51.759
that I saw in Vietnam and
my friend (indistinct)

01:11:51.759 --> 01:11:54.659
are gonna have higher education
the attitude is different.

01:11:57.900 --> 01:12:01.840
And another one is about Western eyes.

01:12:01.840 --> 01:12:06.470
Like, do you think, your Western eyes,

01:12:06.470 --> 01:12:09.280
you see I consider you are Western eyes.

01:12:09.280 --> 01:12:12.360
You look at this as the outsider

01:12:12.360 --> 01:12:14.870
because when I see all of that,

01:12:14.870 --> 01:12:19.560
I explain it in different
way in another way,

01:12:19.560 --> 01:12:22.890
because like Diu-Huong and many other

01:12:22.890 --> 01:12:26.310
talk about the regional differences.

01:12:26.310 --> 01:12:27.710
I have two brother in Lao

01:12:27.710 --> 01:12:30.023
who are from the center of Vietnam.

01:12:32.853 --> 01:12:36.160
And also talk about the doctor in Lao

01:12:38.107 --> 01:12:40.670
taking care of the mother-in-law,

01:12:40.670 --> 01:12:44.530
because the culture in the center

01:12:44.530 --> 01:12:48.713
like on the property of the
family will give to the son.

01:12:49.850 --> 01:12:53.260
So they expect the
daughter-in-law to take care

01:12:53.260 --> 01:12:57.100
while other family, they
even to share with others.

01:12:57.100 --> 01:13:00.520
So usually sometime the
daughter, not the daughter-in-law

01:13:00.520 --> 01:13:03.820
take care of their
parents when they're sick.

01:13:03.820 --> 01:13:08.820
So when we look at that,
we think about culture

01:13:09.860 --> 01:13:14.440
and we see it's very normal
and we think about property,

01:13:14.440 --> 01:13:18.220
maybe I give my property to you

01:13:18.220 --> 01:13:23.150
and you have to take care of
me, it's your responsibility

01:13:23.150 --> 01:13:25.920
something like that, because
we live in that condition.

01:13:25.920 --> 01:13:29.540
But under your Western
eyes, it's not correct,

01:13:29.540 --> 01:13:32.790
when you just look at how the daughter,

01:13:32.790 --> 01:13:35.193
the women taking care of their parents,

01:13:36.230 --> 01:13:40.270
and that's about to be
like doing by nurses,

01:13:40.270 --> 01:13:45.270
or is it just, through your explanation,

01:13:45.410 --> 01:13:47.880
I'm not sure I got it but
through your explanation

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:52.800
is something not right in Western culture

01:13:52.800 --> 01:13:57.586
in Western contexts, are like somehow...

01:13:57.586 --> 01:14:01.170
So do you think that like your Western eye

01:14:01.170 --> 01:14:03.730
affect your explanations?

01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:05.370
That's it.

01:14:05.370 --> 01:14:09.850
- I am sure that my Western
eyes affect my explanations.

01:14:09.850 --> 01:14:13.370
I don't think I was trying to criticize

01:14:13.370 --> 01:14:17.600
the system of relations,
quite the opposite.

01:14:17.600 --> 01:14:20.930
I was saying that this
is the way it works,

01:14:20.930 --> 01:14:23.640
and a lot of times it works quite well.

01:14:23.640 --> 01:14:27.350
And I think what it does
help us understand though

01:14:27.350 --> 01:14:32.350
is also why does social
reproduction continue?

01:14:33.666 --> 01:14:38.420
And a lot of times it continues
not through oppression,

01:14:38.420 --> 01:14:42.320
It continues because people
participate in a system.

01:14:42.320 --> 01:14:43.920
We collude in a system

01:14:43.920 --> 01:14:46.123
and that's not necessarily a bad thing,

01:14:47.440 --> 01:14:50.680
it is the way that things are

01:14:50.680 --> 01:14:54.290
whether we wanna come in and criticize

01:14:54.290 --> 01:14:59.253
that's a different point, but
I'm not sure that we must.

01:15:01.120 --> 01:15:03.350
What I found is that I think

01:15:03.350 --> 01:15:07.700
because this was a generation
pre the family planning,

01:15:07.700 --> 01:15:11.560
and so the people who were
adult and in a position to care

01:15:11.560 --> 01:15:14.410
they oftentimes had multiple siblings.

01:15:14.410 --> 01:15:18.940
And so care was distributed

01:15:18.940 --> 01:15:21.900
among daughters and daughters-in-law.

01:15:21.900 --> 01:15:24.850
It wasn't just daughters-in-law,
it wasn't just daughters,

01:15:24.850 --> 01:15:26.763
it wasn't just hired help.

01:15:27.700 --> 01:15:30.500
The women, I think if you
saw in some of the pictures

01:15:30.500 --> 01:15:34.720
these are not necessarily
uneducated women.

01:15:34.720 --> 01:15:37.470
What I am saying is that a lot of times

01:15:37.470 --> 01:15:42.160
for the worship rituals
and even needing to care

01:15:42.160 --> 01:15:44.623
of the mother-in-law, who was fallen sick,

01:15:45.530 --> 01:15:47.690
they had to suspend their work,

01:15:47.690 --> 01:15:52.690
they had to take time off
and come and take care,

01:15:53.950 --> 01:15:58.490
and so that's where there
is a kind of burden.

01:15:58.490 --> 01:16:01.770
I also found that men, oftentimes
it didn't really matter

01:16:01.770 --> 01:16:04.140
if they're drinking with
their brothers-in-law

01:16:04.140 --> 01:16:07.113
or they're drinking with their
brothers, they enjoyed it.

01:16:08.640 --> 01:16:12.630
And it was oftentimes
sisters and sisters-in-law

01:16:12.630 --> 01:16:16.300
who would do the job of
cleaning up behind them

01:16:17.230 --> 01:16:21.190
and of putting together
the system of relations.

01:16:21.190 --> 01:16:26.190
But I don't think that
it's necessarily bad,

01:16:27.130 --> 01:16:28.523
it's just different.

01:16:30.943 --> 01:16:35.943
And I did see one case of a family

01:16:36.710 --> 01:16:38.990
that was particularly well-off.

01:16:38.990 --> 01:16:41.910
And in that case, the mom actually,

01:16:41.910 --> 01:16:44.710
she goes to an ancestral worship ritual

01:16:44.710 --> 01:16:47.330
and I talk about it in chapter two.

01:16:47.330 --> 01:16:50.420
So she does kind of the
duty, but nominally,

01:16:50.420 --> 01:16:51.830
and then she has to run off to work

01:16:51.830 --> 01:16:54.910
because she has like
an important position.

01:16:54.910 --> 01:16:58.960
And here it is, her
sisters, her hired help,

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:03.660
the sisters-in-law who do the
work of putting on the event

01:17:03.660 --> 01:17:06.940
and her husband comes in
and worships in her stead,

01:17:06.940 --> 01:17:11.190
so there is always, at least
somebody from the family.

01:17:11.190 --> 01:17:14.690
By one say, it's just
women doing all the work

01:17:14.690 --> 01:17:17.750
and I wouldn't say that
it's necessarily bad

01:17:17.750 --> 01:17:22.740
that women do end up doing
a disproportionate amount

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:25.810
of that kind of work, men also are saddled

01:17:25.810 --> 01:17:29.513
with a lot of duties and
burdens and so forth.

01:17:31.090 --> 01:17:33.400
But I'm sure that if I wasn't Vietnamese,

01:17:33.400 --> 01:17:37.760
I mean if I was Vietnamese,
I would see so much more,

01:17:37.760 --> 01:17:40.380
I would hear so much
more, I would understand.

01:17:40.380 --> 01:17:43.340
I mean, obviously I'm
not a native speaker,

01:17:43.340 --> 01:17:47.030
and so I'm sure I missed a lot.

01:17:47.030 --> 01:17:50.690
And I'm sure that if I
had grown up in Vietnam

01:17:52.240 --> 01:17:53.210
there was a good chance

01:17:53.210 --> 01:17:56.643
I'd be seeing things
differently than I did.

01:17:59.720 --> 01:18:01.040
- Thank you Merav,

01:18:01.040 --> 01:18:04.090
and thank you everyone for
your wonderful questions

01:18:04.090 --> 01:18:07.850
and for joining us for the talk today.

01:18:07.850 --> 01:18:09.470
I'm gonna bring it to a close now

01:18:09.470 --> 01:18:12.920
but I'm gonna stop the record
here, but please join me

01:18:12.920 --> 01:18:16.103
in thanking Merav for a
wonderful talk and discussion.

